    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "It is well-known that compaction bands (CBs), joints and faults are often present in the same rock volume in the Jurassic eolian Aztec Sandstone, exposed at the Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada, USA. Because the permeabilities of these structures may be a few orders of magnitude different from each other and from the average permeability of the matrix, it is desirable to evaluate their combined effects on fluid flow. In this study, we investigate their effects by performing permeability upscaling over a grid-sized area/volume populated by a variety of common structures in reservoir simulations. Through investigating the problems of two sets of vertical CBs, an inclined CB set intersecting a vertical CB set, a joint set intersecting a CB set, and a small fault overprinting a CB set, our numerical modeling results demonstrate the importance of considering and accurately representing all these structures in fluid flow models. For instance, the intersection angle of two sets of structures may have a significant impact on the magnitude and orientation of the principal effective permeability. Waterflood simulations are also performed in which the rock volumes are initially saturated with oil. We find that waterflood is an effective approach to recover oil when various structures are present together in the Aztec Sandstone, but the relative orientations and locations of these structures have certain influence on the efficiency of the oil recovery.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
